pression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186416323 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\hat{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\