.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."] [17592186416357 "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186416358 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\hat{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\hat{x}) & =‖x-\\hat{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\hat{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\hat{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\hat{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2-0)^{2}+(6-0)^{2}+(2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+36+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{44} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186416359 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$  \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}\\sqrt{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."] [17592186416397 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -2 & -1 & -3\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & -3 & -2 & -6\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=2{R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 4 & 6\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & 2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-5,$ $b=3,$ $c=2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=-5+3+2=0.\n$$"] [17592186416399 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}50 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}6 & 4 & 50 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 & -20\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{25}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 & -20\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{25}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{10}{3} & \\frac{50}{3} & -\\frac{50}{3}\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{10}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{25}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -5\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -5\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186416979 "The Hadamard product of two compatible vectors $u$ and $v$ can be expressed as the matrix product\n\n$$\nv⊙u=\\text{diag}(v)u.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nBy=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(AB)\\cdot (By) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\text{diag}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix})\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}⊙\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186417740 "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is ${x}_{1}$, and we have one free variable, ${x}_{2}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ in terms of ${x}_{2}.$\n\nFrom the first equation we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2},$ so the set of all the solutions of $Ax=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}.\n$$"] [17592186417869 "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3\\cdot (2{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=5{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{(-9)\\cdot 5+(-5)\\cdot 2+(-5)\\cdot 1}{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{-60\\,}{\\,\\,\\,30\\,}b \\\\  & =-2b \\\\  & =-2\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186419579 "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nSetting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=-2y+z.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2y+z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,y,z\\in R\\}.$"] [17592186421783 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}2uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2v \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2v \\\\ 1 & 2u\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2v \\\\ 0 & 2u\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2v,\\,-2u,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨{x}^{2},\\,xy,\\,z⟩{|}_{x=u\\,y=v\\,z=2uv} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2},\\,uv,\\,2uv⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨{u}^{2},\\,uv,\\,2uv⟩\\cdot ⟨-2v,\\,-2u,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot (-2v)+uv\\cdot (-2u)+2uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2{u}^{2}v-2{u}^{2}v+2uv \\\\  & =2uv-4{u}^{2}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\cdot uv+(-4)\\cdot {u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=2-4=-2.\n$$"] [17592186421785 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-1,\\,2u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2u \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2u \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2u \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2u,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨3z,\\,-2x,\\,-y⟩|}_{x=-v\\,y=-u\\,z={u}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨3{u}^{2},\\,-2(-v),\\,-(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{u}^{2},\\,2v,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3{u}^{2},\\,2v,\\,u⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-2u,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot 0+2v\\cdot (-2u)+u\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-4uv-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3z,\\,-2x,\\,-y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4uv-u)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4uv-u)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4uv+u)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2{u}^{2}v+\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2v+\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =-{[{v}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}v]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186423120 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-u-v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,-2\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2}-{z}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=-u-v} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}-(-u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(-u-v)^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}-(-u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(-u-v)^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\cdot (1)+({v}^{2}-(-u-v)^{2})\\cdot (1)+(-u-v)^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}+{v}^{2}-(-u-v)^{2}+(-u-v)^{2} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2}-{z}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\color{blue}{u}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186423123 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,-3\\le v\\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨(x+y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(x-y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=v-u} \\\\  & =⟨(u+v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨(u+v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(u+v)^{2}\\cdot 1+(u-v)^{2}\\cdot (-1)+uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨(x+y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(x-y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5uv)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{\\color{blue}5uv}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186423124 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u-3v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}3,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}2z,\\hspace{0.222em}y+z⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=u-3v} \\\\  & =⟨u+u-3v,\\hspace{0.222em}2(u-3v),\\hspace{0.222em}v+u-3v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u-3v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-6v,\\hspace{0.222em}u-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨2u-3v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-6v,\\hspace{0.222em}u-2v⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}3,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(2u-3v)\\cdot (-1)+(2u-6v)\\cdot (3)+(u-2v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2u+3v+6u-18v+u-2v \\\\  & =5u-17v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z-y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y-z,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5u-17v)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\color{blue}(5u-17v)}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186423620 "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(V),$ we reduce the matrix $V$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"] [17592186424594 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 & 15 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.\n$$"] [17592186425337 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =2,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t+1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c9/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ y &=2(t+1){e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.37$ | $0$ | $0.60$ |\n| $y$ | $0.37$ | $2$ | $3.58$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/42/4232a3293f2414f6121c834fa09145f5f3b573d83ad7a3695c6ca93c228c8563.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/9e/9e78fbd54f2825d28581b8181cab7762b7a625f7b21620eec37436771c9e1695.png)"] [17592186427559 "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 8 & -16 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"]}
